  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure dominates through Saturday yielding \ndry conditions and above normal temperatures.\n\nA building upper level ridge over the central CONUS will continue to \nshift eastward through the end of the week. At the surface, large \nand expansive high pressure will meander over the Mid-Atlantic \nregion through Friday before shifting offshore on Saturday. \n\nAs a result, mainly clear skies and dry conditions are expected into \nthe start of the weekend. High temperatures will gradually step up \neach day as the upper ridge builds overhead with highs reaching into \nthe mid to upper 60s by Friday. This is as much as 10-15 degrees \nabove normal for mid-November! Lows will typically be in the 30s for \nthe majority of the area, with 40s for the immediate Philadelphia \nmetro and for coastal areas. The upper level ridge will then shift \noffshore on Saturday, where temps will still be above normal, but a \nbit cooler than Friday. Overall, a pleasant stretch of weather ahead.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring a potential coastal low for the second \nhalf of the weekend into early next week. \n\nAfter surface high pressure shifts offshore on Saturday, most \nguidance depict a coastal low developing off the coast of the \nSoutheast US over the weekend. While there are great differences \namongst timing, track and intensity of this potential system, there \nis a general consensus in a low developing. In fact, AI guidance has \nbeen fairly consistent with the low tracking over/within close \nvicinity of the Mid-Atlantic while other ensemble guidance keeps the \nsystem well offshore. While it is well too early for specifics of \nwhat 'potential' impacts may be felt around the region, it is worth \nwatching as this may be the next chance to bring some beneficial \nrainfall to the region. PoPs are currently around 20-40%.",
